    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chenla is a Chinese label: most historians now hold that it was never a unitary kingdom but a series of loose and temporary confederations of Khmer principalities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-vickery">Michael Vickery</a>'s 'Dângrêk Chieftains'.</li><li>Isanavarman I, whose embassy reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> court in 616–17 and whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambor-prei-kuk">Isanapura</a> (Sambor Prei Kuk), absorbed the territories of Funan and called himself King of Kings ruling 'Suvarnabhumi as far as the sea'.</li><li>After 707 the Tang histories divide Chenla into Land and Water Chenla; Water Chenla fell to the Javanese around 790, while from Land Chenla <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> proclaimed the Khmer Empire in 802.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Judith Jacob found fourteen categories of slaves in Chenla's inscriptions, distinguished by origin and duty; they could be bought, sold and given away, bound by their parents' debts or born into bondage.</p></aside>
    </section>

